A||N||4||bwayne||heavy clutch question||||5||
Z||000000||bwayne||02-09-2000||01:13 PM||||At the risk of sounding stupid, is there a way to reduce the effort needed to depress the clutch in my stock 88 Formula?  I have an 85 coupe w/V8, Isuzu 5spd & Centerforce dual friction clutch that shifts much, much easier than the stock car.  As I drive the 88 more, I'd like it to be easier, too.  Is there something wrong, or is there a way to adjust the pressure?  TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||02-09-2000||01:30 PM||||The only way to lighten the clutch, would be to change out the pressure plate. But that would include a matching clutch set. I went with a RAM clutch set, and it made the pedal effort in my Formula a lttle lighter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||02-09-2000||07:10 PM||||Get some pedal covers.  Yes I know some think that they are tacky or form w/o function.  I thought that too until I got some for my birthday.  1) They look cool (personal opinion), 2) easier heal and toe, since they will be closer due to there larger size 3) since they are larger than the factory pedals, the force per cubic inch is dissipated.  This won't actually make the force required to depress the clutch less, but it is sure easier on the foot, and does “kind of feel” easier to push in the clutch.||||reg||7
Z||000003||fiero56||02-09-2000||07:32 PM||||I've got a set of pedal covers on my Fiero and like them. I had the problem of everytime my shoes were wet from snow/rain they would slip off the pedals going from a stop or shifting. Not all the time, but often enough to annoy me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||02-10-2000||05:42 PM||||bwayne, you just answered your own question.  I recently put a centerforce dual friction clutch in my 88GT and have experienced a significant decrease in pedal effort.  The only way to correct the "force" needed to depress it, is to buy another centerforce.  Sorry, but there's no easy or cheap way around it.||||reg||1
